{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":7982170,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"gardening\",\"short_title\":\"organic-mulch-guide-vegetable-gardens-2026\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-08-08 18:14:17\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-08-08 18:14:17\",\"author\":\"David Park\",\"img\":null,\"title\":\"Best Organic Mulch Types for Vegetable Gardens\",\"description\":\"Compare organic mulch types for vegetable gardens. Learn when to use straw, leaves, grass clippings, and wood chips.\",\"url\":null,\"body\":\"<h2>Why Mulch Matters in Vegetable Gardens</h2>\\n<p>Mulch is one of the most impactful soil amendments a gardener can apply. A 2-3 inch layer of organic mulch suppresses weeds by blocking sunlight, reduces water evaporation by up to 70 percent, moderates soil temperature, and breaks down over time to feed soil organisms. The right mulch choice depends on your crop type, climate, and gardening goals.</p>\\n<h2>Straw Mulch</h2>\\n<p>Straw is the gold standard for vegetable gardens. It is lightweight, easy to spread, and breaks down at a moderate pace. Use straw — not hay, which contains weed seeds. Apply 3-4 inches around tomatoes, peppers, and squash. Straw keeps soil-borne diseases from splashing onto lower leaves during rain, which is especially important for preventing early blight on tomatoes.</p>\\n<h2>Shredded Leaf Mulch</h2>\\n<p>Free and abundant in fall, shredded leaves make excellent mulch. Run a lawn mower over fallen leaves to shred them — whole leaves mat together and block water. Shredded leaves add organic matter and attract earthworms. Best applied at 2-3 inches around cool-season crops like kale, spinach, and carrots.</p>\\n<h2>Grass Clipping Mulch</h2>\\n<p>Fresh grass clippings are nitrogen-rich and break down quickly. Apply in thin layers (no more than 1 inch at a time) to avoid creating a slimy, anaerobic mat. Only use clippings from lawns that have not been treated with herbicides within the past 6 weeks. Ideal for heavy-feeding crops like corn and squash.</p>\\n<h2>Wood Chip Mulch</h2>\\n<p>Best for pathways and around perennial crops like asparagus and rhubarb. Fresh wood chips temporarily tie up surface nitrogen as they decompose, so avoid placing them directly against annual vegetable stems. Aged or composted wood chips are safer for active growing areas. Wood chips last 2-3 seasons and create excellent habitat for beneficial fungi.</p>\\n<h2>Pine Needle Mulch</h2>\\n<p>Pine needles (pine straw) are lightweight and allow water to penetrate easily. They slightly acidify soil as they decompose, making them ideal for acid-loving plants like blueberries and potatoes. Spread 2-3 inches deep and refresh annually. Pine needles interlock on slopes, making them effective for erosion control.</p>\\n<h2>Mulch Application Tips</h2>\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Timing</strong>: Apply after soil has warmed in late spring. Mulching too early delays warming for heat-loving crops.</li>\\n<li><strong>Depth</strong>: 2-3 inches for most materials. Deeper layers can suffocate roots and block water.</li>\\n<li><strong>Stem clearance</strong>: Keep mulch 2-3 inches away from plant stems to prevent rot and rodent damage.</li>\\n<li><strong>Pre-mulch prep</strong>: Water thoroughly and pull existing weeds before applying mulch.</li>\\n</ul>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":null,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-08 18:14:17\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}